Everybody called her Aspirin -- she always looked like she was aspiring to become a special state of being, and just looking at her made your headache go away.

She earned the nickname back in our sophomore year of college, during a particularly brutal week of midterms. I was hunched over a macroeconomics textbook in the campus library, nursing a tension headache that felt like a tight band of steel around my skull, when she dropped into the chair across from me. She didn't know me. I didn't know her. She simply looked at my pile of highlighters, my empty coffee cups, and the expression on my face, and said, "You need to take a walk."

"I don't have time," I muttered.

"That's exactly why you have time," she replied, with a certainty so calm and absolute that it felt like a physical force. I closed my book. We walked around the quad for twenty minutes, and she told me about the mating habits of octopuses — a topic so bizarre and fascinating that my headache simply packed its bags and left before we made it back to the library steps.

Her real name was Alice Peng, but the nickname stuck so fast that even professors began using it. "Ask Aspirin," people would say whenever a problem arose, and somehow, she always had a way of dissolving the tension.

She was not beautiful in the way magazines would recognize. She had a round face, slightly uneven eyebrows, and a habit of tucking her hair behind her left ear only, which gave her a perpetually lopsided look. But there was something about her presence — a quality of attention, perhaps — that made you feel like the most interesting person in the room. When she listened, she *listened*, with her whole body leaning forward, her dark eyes steady, as if whatever you were saying was being recorded for posterity.

After college, we stayed in touch the way people do — loosely, intermittently, with long gaps that felt like nothing because whenever we reconnected, the conversation resumed mid-sentence. She became a social worker in a small city upstate. I took a marketing job in Chicago. We exchanged texts about mundane things: the quality of a sandwich, the behavior of a coworker, the particular shade of orange the sky turned at 6:47 p.m. in autumn.

It was during one of those gaps that I got the call.

"Stage three," she said, on a Tuesday afternoon in October. Her voice was the same as always — measured, warm, faintly amused. "Ovarian. The kind that doesn't ask permission."

I sat down on my kitchen floor, because there was no chair close enough. "Aspirin," I said, and then I couldn't say anything else.

"See?" she said. "Even *I* can't fix this one."

---

The months that followed were a study in reversal. I drove up every other weekend. I sat with her through chemotherapy sessions in rooms that smelled of antiseptic and stale hope. I watched her hair thin and then go, watched the lopsided look become more pronounced as her face narrowed. She wore a knit cap she called her "thinking beanie" and refused to buy a wig on the grounds that wigs were "for people who wanted to pretend, and I've never been good at pretending."

The cancer did what cancer does — it took and took, and then it took some more. But something strange happened in the process. The more it took from her body, the more she seemed to become that thing she had always been aspiring to. There was a clarity to her, a transparency, as if the illness were burning away everything nonessential and leaving only the purest version of who she was.

She started writing letters. Not emails — actual letters, on paper, in her looping handwriting. She wrote to her mother, to old friends, to a high school teacher she hadn't spoken to in fifteen years. She wrote to me, even though I was right there.

"You're the only person who ever let me finish my sentences," she wrote in one. "Do you know how rare that is? Most people are just waiting for their turn to talk. You actually *heard* me. That's worth more than any medicine."

I kept that letter in my wallet, and when the weight of missing her became a physical ache, I would take it out and read it again, and it was almost like having her there — almost like the headache lifting.

---

She died on a Thursday in March, during the first warm day of the year. I remember thinking it was exactly the kind of day she would have chosen — the kind where the air smells like thawing earth and possibility, where you can feel the world deciding to try again.

At her memorial service, I met people I had only heard about in her stories. Her mother, small and fierce, gripped my hand and said, "You're the one she called her aspirin." I must have looked confused, because she added, "She told me you cured her headaches. She never told you?"

I shook my head. I hadn't known. I hadn't known that the nickname ran both ways, that in the private mythology of her life, I had been the one who made her pain go away.

There were maybe forty people at the service, and every single one of them had a version of the same story — a moment when Alice Peng had appeared out of nowhere, said exactly the right thing, and made a problem dissolve. A woman who said Aspirin had talked her out of dropping out of graduate school. A man who said she had sat with him in an emergency room for six hours after his wife's accident and never once checked her phone. A teenager who said she had been the only adult who ever told him the truth about how hard life could be, and then immediately followed it with how worth it was.

I stood up to speak, and for the first time in my life, I understood what she had meant about aspiring to become a special state of being. It wasn't about achieving something or becoming someone. It was about the quality of your presence — the way you showed up, fully, for the brief time you were here.

"Everybody called her Aspirin," I said, "because she made your headache go away. But the truth is, she didn't take the pain. She just helped you remember that you were strong enough to carry it."

---

That was three years ago. I still keep her letter in my wallet, though the folds are wearing thin and the ink is fading. Sometimes, when I'm on the train or standing in line at the grocery store, I'll see someone who looks tired and overwhelmed, and I'll hear her voice in my head: *You need to take a walk.*

So I'll say something — small, ordinary, maybe a little strange. I'll tell them about the mating habits of octopuses, or the exact shade of orange the sky turns at 6:47 in autumn. And for a moment, just a moment, I'll see their expression shift, and I'll know that the headache has lifted.

It's the closest I can come to becoming what she was. And maybe that's the point — maybe that's what she was aspiring to all along. Not to become something special herself, but to show the rest of us that we already were. [end of text]


